##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    1931 1451  218
                                                                  1931 1451  219
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       1931 1451  220
                                                                  1931 1451  221
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          1931 1451  222
                                                                  1931 1451  223
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1931 1451  224
                                                                  1931 1451  225
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1931 1451  226
                                                                  1931 1451  227
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1931 1451  228
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1931 1451  229
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1931 1451  230
                                                                  1931 1451  231
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1931 1451  232
                                                                  1931 1451  233
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1931 1451  234
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1931 1451  235
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1931 1451  236
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1931 1451  237
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1931 1451  238
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1931 1451  239
to the highest incident energy.                                   1931 1451  240
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1931 1451  241
                                                                  1931 1451  242
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1931 1451  243
                                                                  1931 1451  244
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1931 1451  245
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1931 1451  246
                                                                  1931 1451  247
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1931 1451  248
                                                                  1931 1451  249
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1931 1451  250
total cross section.                                              1931 1451  251
                                                                  1931 1451  252
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1931 1451  253
                                                                  1931 1451  254
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1931 1451  255
                                                                  1931 1451  256
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1931 1451  257
                                                                  1931 1451  258
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1931 1451  259
                                                                  1931 1451  260
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1931 1451  261
                                                                  1931 1451  262
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1931 1451  263
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1931 1451  264
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1931 1451  265
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1931 1451  266
sections.                                                         1931 1451  267
                                                                  1931 1451  268
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1931 1451  269
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1931 1451  270
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1931 1451  271
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1931 1451  272
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1931 1451  273
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1931 1451  274
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1931 1451  275
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1931 1451  276
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1931 1451  277
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1931 1451  278
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1931 1451  279
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1931 1451  280
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1931 1451  281
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1931 1451  282
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1931 1451  283
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1931 1451  284
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1931 1451  285
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1931 1451  286
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1931 1451  287
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1931 1451  288
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1931 1451  289
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1931 1451  290
                                                                  1931 1451  291
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1931 1451  292
                                                                  1931 1451  293
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1931 1451  294
discrete levels.                                                  1931 1451  295
                                                                  1931 1451  296
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1931 1451  297
                                                                  1931 1451  298
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1931 1451  299
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1931 1451  300
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1931 1451  301
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1931 1451  302
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1931 1451  303
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1931 1451  304
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1931 1451  305
on an angular grid.                                               1931 1451  306
                                                                  1931 1451  307
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1931 1451  308
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1931 1451  309
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1931 1451  310
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1931 1451  311
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1931 1451  312
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1931 1451  313
                                                                  1931 1451  314
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1931 1451  315
                                                                  1931 1451  316
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1931 1451  317
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1931 1451  318
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1931 1451  319
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1931 1451  320
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1931 1451  321
                                                                  1931 1451  322
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1931 1451  323
                                                                  1931 1451  324
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1931 1451  325
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1931 1451  326
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1931 1451  327
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1931 1451  328
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1931 1451  329
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1931 1451  330
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1931 1451  331
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1931 1451  332
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1931 1451  333
For each residual product:                                        1931 1451  334
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1931 1451  335
- the recoil                                                      1931 1451  336
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1931 1451  337
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1931 1451  338
sections.                                                         1931 1451  339
                                                                  1931 1451  340
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1931 1451  341
                                                                  1931 1451  342
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1931 1451  343
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1931 1451  344
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1931 1451  345
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1931 1451  346
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1931 1451  347
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1931 1451  348
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1931 1451  349
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1931 1451  350
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1931 1451  351
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1931 1451  352
                                                                  1931 1451  353
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1931 1451  354
followed:                                                         1931 1451  355
                                                                  1931 1451  356
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1931 1451  357
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1931 1451  358
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1931 1451  359
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1931 1451  360
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1931 1451  361
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1931 1451  362
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1931 1451  363
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1931 1451  364
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1931 1451  365
                                                                  1931 1451  366
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1931 1451  367
                                                                  1931 1451  368
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1931 1451  369
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1931 1451  370
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1931 1451  371
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1931 1451  372
                                                                  1931 1451  373
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1931 1451  374
                                                                  1931 1451  375
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1931 1451  376
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1931 1451  377
as isomer.                                                        1931 1451  378
                                                                  1931 1451  379
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1931 1451  380
                                                                  1931 1451  381
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1931 1451  382
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1931 1451  383
as isomer.                                                        1931 1451  384
                                                                  1931 1451  385
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1931 1451  386
                                                                  1931 1451  387
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1931 1451  388
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1931 1451  389
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1931 1451  390
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1931 1451  391
  given                                                           1931 1451  392
                                                                  1931 1451  393
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1931 1451  394
                                                                  1931 1451  395
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1931 1451  396
                                                                  1931 1451  397
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1931 1451  398
                                                                  1931 1451  399
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1931 1451  400
                                                                  1931 1451  401
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1931 1451  402
                                                                  1931 1451  403
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1931 1451  404
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1931 1451  405
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1931 1451  406
                                                                  1931 1451  407
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1931 1451  408
                                                                  1931 1451  409
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1931 1451  410
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1931 1451  411
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1931 1451  412
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1931 1451  413
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1931 1451  414
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1931 1451  415
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1931 1451  416
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1931 1451  417
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1931 1451  418
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1931 1451  419
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1931 1451  420
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1931 1451  421
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1931 1451  422
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1931 1451  423
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1931 1451  424
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1931 1451  425
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1931 1451  426
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1931 1451  427
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1931 1451  428
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1931 1451  429
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1931 1451  430
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1931 1451  431
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1931 1451  432
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1931 1451  433
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1931 1451  434
                                                                  1931 1451  435
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1931 1451  436
                                                                  1931 1451  437
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1931 1451  438
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1931 1451  439
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1931 1451  440
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1931 1451  441
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1931 1451  442
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1931 1451  443
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1931 1451  444
representation is used.                                           1931 1451  445
                                                                  1931 1451  446
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1931 1451  447
                                                                  1931 1451  448
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1931 1451  449
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1931 1451  450
                                                                  1931 1451  451
